WEBVTT

c0
00:00:03.349 --> 00:00:06.185
In class, it's not so
bad, but when school's

c1
00:00:06.269 --> 00:00:08.569
out and the others go
off to enjoy themselves,

c2
00:00:08.670 --> 00:00:12.140
well, if you're what they call a shy guy,

c3
00:00:12.470 --> 00:00:14.029
that's when you really feel it.

c4
00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:17.149
The odd loneliness of being new in town.

c5
00:00:17.704 --> 00:00:19.805
You don't know how to
make people like you,

c6
00:00:19.889 --> 00:00:22.489
and you find yourself holding
a grudge against them.

c7
00:00:23.209 --> 00:00:25.590
You're standing on the outside looking in.

c8
00:00:26.265 --> 00:00:29.266
You might have something to
contribute to their conversation,

c9
00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.301
but nobody cares whether you do or not.

c10
00:00:31.989 --> 00:00:36.240
There's a barrier, and you don't
know how to begin breaking it down.

c11
00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.500
You imagine they keep watching the
way you look, the way you act.

c12
00:00:41.069 --> 00:00:47.099
They think you're different, so
you head for home. What else?

c13
00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:52.240
But still, you can't forget
that you're alone, an outsider.

c14
00:00:56.439 --> 00:00:58.549
Hello Phil. How's everything?

c15
00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.110
OK, I guess, Dad, your record
transmitter and microphone coming.

c16
00:01:03.409 --> 00:01:04.560
Oh, all right, I guess.

c17
00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:06.889
I know it isn't either.

c18
00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.801
I can't figure out where to
connect in the oscillator.

c19
00:01:09.885 --> 00:01:12.186
I know I have to connect
it into the amplifier,

c20
00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:13.971
but where, it just doesn't fit in.

c21
00:01:14.230 --> 00:01:16.631
Why not go to the store
and get more information?

c22
00:01:17.029 --> 00:01:19.750
And by the way, how are
things going in school?

c23
00:01:20.239 --> 00:01:22.050
Oh, OK, Dad.

c24
00:01:22.889 --> 00:01:25.809
But school here isn't like
it was back in Morristown.

c25
00:01:26.169 --> 00:01:29.550
Well, you know, son, maybe
school is like your radio.

c26
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:33.569
This oscillator will do its
work well, but as you said,

c27
00:01:33.809 --> 00:01:37.989
you still have to fit it in so it
can work with all the other parts.

c28
00:01:38.290 --> 00:01:43.510
I know what you're driving at, Dad, but I
don't think I ever will fit in, not here.

c29
00:01:43.839 --> 00:01:45.890
I'm different from the guys in this town.

c30
00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:49.000
Well, as far as that goes,
Phil, everybody's different.

c31
00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:51.669
That's what makes people
interesting. Maybe.

c32
00:01:52.889 --> 00:01:56.480
You know, When we moved here this summer,

c33
00:01:56.639 --> 00:01:59.316
I had quite a time making friends in a new

c34
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:01.601
office where everybody
else knew each other.

c35
00:02:01.919 --> 00:02:05.459
What'd you do, Dad? Well,
that's beside the point, son.

c36
00:02:05.699 --> 00:02:08.500
What works for one doesn't
necessarily work for another,

c37
00:02:09.059 --> 00:02:12.296
but I bet the other men didn't
look at your clothes all the time.

c38
00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.131
Well, what makes you think
they'll look at your clothes?

c39
00:02:15.215 --> 00:02:19.000
Oh, because the other fellows
wear sweaters or just shirts,

c40
00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:20.990
not a regular suit like mine.

c41
00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:23.139
Well, wear a sweater then.

c42
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:24.701
The clothes alone won't.

c43
00:02:26.289 --> 00:02:29.059
Phil, what about the other fellows?

c44
00:02:29.449 --> 00:02:30.619
What do they do?

c45
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:32.850
What do they like?

c46
00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:35.830
Gosh, Dad, I don't know. I never noticed.

c47
00:02:36.850 --> 00:02:40.970
-Only I know it's not radios. -Perhaps
that's the reason you don't know.

c48
00:02:41.289 --> 00:02:45.089
You don't notice what the others are
interested in. Why not try to find out?

c49
00:02:45.449 --> 00:02:46.450
How, Dad?

c50
00:02:47.089 --> 00:02:50.649
Well, you might try watching, listening.

c51
00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:55.139
Pick out the most popular boys and girls
in school and keep an eye on them.

c52
00:02:57.289 --> 00:02:59.929
Trying to figure out why people like them.

c53
00:03:00.669 --> 00:03:04.520
Not that you'll ever be just like any
of them, but you might learn something.

c54
00:03:05.479 --> 00:03:06.800
Well, see you at dinner.

c55
00:03:12.449 --> 00:03:15.589
Well, there's an idea, maybe a good idea,

c56
00:03:16.130 --> 00:03:17.369
worth a try anyway.

c57
00:03:18.080 --> 00:03:20.419
And tomorrow is not too soon to start.

c58
00:03:24.929 --> 00:03:28.679
Pick out the most popular boys and girls
in school and keep an eye on them.

c59
00:03:29.979 --> 00:03:33.630
Who are they now? Some of them
are right here in this class.

c60
00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:36.839
Andy McIntyre, for instance.

c61
00:03:37.779 --> 00:03:39.080
People like him all right.

c62
00:03:40.839 --> 00:03:43.089
Chick Gallagher rates
high in popularity too.

c63
00:03:44.910 --> 00:03:46.389
There's Jane Davenport.

c64
00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:48.770
She's popular with boys and girls.

c65
00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:51.190
And Jack Gilbert

c66
00:03:52.309 --> 00:03:55.089
What's he got that makes
people like him so much?

c67
00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:05.421
Andy's got something to
say and Jack's listening.

c68
00:04:06.169 --> 00:04:10.535
He really seems interested. Come
to think of it, Jack's always

c69
00:04:10.619 --> 00:04:12.820
interested in what people
are talking about.

c70
00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.110
Maybe that's why he hits it
off so well with everybody.

c71
00:04:16.709 --> 00:04:18.730
Anyhow, Andy thinks he's a great guy.

c72
00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:26.480
No, that's too easy, just
listening. But what's this?

c73
00:04:28.109 --> 00:04:31.309
Sounds like Jane's caught on to
the same thing. She's listening.

c74
00:04:32.309 --> 00:04:35.750
Hearing about Helen's collection
of menus and liking it.

c75
00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:38.291
That makes your kingpin with Helen.

c76
00:04:39.709 --> 00:04:43.059
Maybe there is something in this
business of being a good listener.

c77
00:04:46.239 --> 00:04:51.076
But still. There must be more
to getting along with people

c78
00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.700
than just wearing a sweater and listening.

c79
00:05:05.540 --> 00:05:09.100
What's the matter, Bob? Handle
stuck? Yeah, I can't get it open.

c80
00:05:09.339 --> 00:05:11.939
Oh, here, let me try. It's
a trick to some of these.

c81
00:05:13.630 --> 00:05:16.350
There. Geez, thanks a lot, Jack.

c82
00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:20.660
Now, why didn't you think of that?

c83
00:05:21.149 --> 00:05:25.339
Chick's always doing things for
people, and it pays off in Friends.

c84
00:05:25.634 --> 00:05:28.685
Boy, that sure is one tough
history assign we have for Friday.

c85
00:05:28.769 --> 00:05:31.470
I wish I could find some good
outside reading material.

c86
00:05:31.554 --> 00:05:35.155
I found a swell book that covers
everything Miss Carson wants us to know.

c87
00:05:35.239 --> 00:05:38.880
It's called the, uh, Last Days
of Monarchism by Henry Darrell.

c88
00:05:39.079 --> 00:05:40.580
You can get it at the library.

c89
00:05:40.664 --> 00:05:43.765
I'm sure glad you told me about
it. I'll get it this afternoon.

c90
00:05:43.849 --> 00:05:45.550
Thanks a lot, Bob. It's OK, chick.

c91
00:05:48.059 --> 00:05:50.750
You dope. You knew about that book.

c92
00:05:51.140 --> 00:05:53.491
You could have been the
guy that helped the guy.

c93
00:05:55.209 --> 00:05:59.450
There it is again. Even when you bump into
people, they hardly know you're alive.

c94
00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:03.481
You know.

c95
00:06:06.679 --> 00:06:07.680
You're alone, bud,

c96
00:06:11.269 --> 00:06:14.920
alone in the crowd, but
keep looking around.

c97
00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:16.891
Try to find out what the score is.

c98
00:06:17.239 --> 00:06:18.940
Look at Jack and Beezy over there.

c99
00:06:19.850 --> 00:06:21.350
Girls seem to like them.

c100
00:06:22.329 --> 00:06:23.589
Why? What's the angle?

c101
00:06:25.019 --> 00:06:26.890
They do act kind of polite.

c102
00:06:27.480 --> 00:06:30.731
At least they seat the girls and
give their orders to the waiters.

c103
00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:33.121
They aren't loud like some of the guys.

c104
00:06:33.775 --> 00:06:37.326
Cherry Coke, chocolate coke, milkshake,
and mola. Double chocolate mole.

c105
00:06:37.410 --> 00:06:38.450
OK, I got 2.

c106
00:06:38.970 --> 00:06:40.606
gee, excuse me, please.

c107
00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:42.341
I've got the orders for my table.

c108
00:06:42.510 --> 00:06:45.926
Uh, just help me out during rush
hour, Bob. Gee, thanks a lot, Chick.

c109
00:06:46.010 --> 00:06:48.059
That's swelling. OK, Bob. Hey

c110
00:06:48.329 --> 00:06:50.589
girl, there's Chick being helpful again.

c111
00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:52.750
I can't quite decide.
